WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:09.010
- So I'm pleased to introduce
our guest, John Kerry

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:13.320
our nation's first Special
Presidential Envoy for Climate

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:14.990
and the first principal

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:16.860
to sit on the National Security Council

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:19.990
dedicated to climate change.

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:22.080
I think you know prior
to his current service

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.680
Secretary Kerry was the Carnegie Endowment

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.821
for International Peace's

00:00:26.821 --> 00:00:29.300
first ever visiting
distinguished statesman.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.320
He was our country's top
diplomat for four years

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:36.720
as the 68th United States,
Secretary of State.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.590
He's a decorated combat veteran

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:41.720
and served 25 years in the U.S. Senate.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:42.630
As many of you know

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.770
Secretary Kerry is a
Distinguished Fellow at Yale

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:47.010
in the Jackson Institute

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:52.010
and a member of the Yale
College Class of 1966.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.510
So Secretary Kerry, thank
you for joining us today.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.950
And we've gathered a lot
of questions for you.

00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:59.233
So I wanna jump right in.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.880
I know you'll be representing
the United States

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:08.480
at the 2021 United Nations
Climate Change Conference

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:09.400
at the end of the year.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.170
And we'd be interested in
hearing what your goals are

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:13.003
for that event.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:15.070
- Well, Peter, first of all,

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:17.200
it's great to be with a Yale Climate Day

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.420
and Michael, thank you.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:21.170
And Peter, thank you for the
invitation to be with you.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:26.970
We are woefully behind
where we ought to be

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.210
on an international basis.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:32.090
In Paris, we all agreed to
keep the Earth's temperature

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:34.670
increase well below two degrees,

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:37.363
and if possible at 1.5 degrees centigrade.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:41.720
And the truth is that we're far from that

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:45.080
we knew in Paris that
even if everybody did

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:45.940
what they were gonna do

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:49.060
we'd still arise in temperature
to about 3.7 degrees.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:50.580
And because we're not doing everything

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.020
people said they would do

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.960
we're actually heading
four degrees or more.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:57.370
So it's quite fundamentally catastrophic.

00:01:57.370 --> 00:02:00.870
On the upside, we have the ability

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:03.040
to avoid the worst
consequences of climate.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:04.890
We really can do this

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:08.370
but the next 10 years
are absolutely critical.

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:13.370
So the goal at Glasgow is to
bring global ambition way up.

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:17.850
We had a summit in the
White House last month

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:19.930
the President Biden hosted,

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:24.930
that summit resulted in
about 55% of global GDP

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:29.130
being committed to keep to the 1.5 degrees

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:31.930
that includes Japan,
Canada, the United States,

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:34.440
the UK, Europe, et cetera

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:37.020
but we're now working with the other 45%.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.610
That's the diplomacy
of the next six months.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:42.560
How do we bring everybody else on board

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.430
to change their national
determined contribution

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:49.200
and DC as it's called raise their ambition

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.340
so that we lay out a
plan, literally a roadmap

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:57.677
for 10 years to get to 2030
with preserving 1.5 degrees

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:04.470
and then building on the
plans to go to 2050 net zero

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:06.580
which more and more
people are now adopting.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:09.090
The problem has Peter we
don't want to be stuck

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:10.450
with a whole bunch of,

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:14.000
wow, we're for 2050 net
zero that's our deal,

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.690
because it doesn't get us there.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:18.810
You've got to get people to
buy in to the next 10 years,

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:22.200
that's the goal of
Glasgow, that and finance

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:24.000
and then what's called the rule book.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.060
We need to kind of close out the rules

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:29.353
by which we're gonna implement
this over the next years.

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:33.820
- You know you're mentioning
the setting of goals

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:37.970
and the long-term
orientation that many have

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:41.790
when we need a decade of
this decade orientation

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:47.220
but it's true that with
your help many institutions,

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:51.380
many companies, financial companies,

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:55.720
others universities as
well have made pledges

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:59.700
to reduce admissions and have
set goals for themselves.

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:04.280
How can we be sure, how
can we have confidence

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:06.870
that there are concrete paths
toward meeting this goals

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:10.700
and that these institutions
actually put them in place

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:13.410
rather than just put the goal on a sign

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:14.990
and hope for the best?

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:18.650
- Well that's partly the rule
book that I just talked about

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:23.650
which is going to require updated data.

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:27.790
But secondly, we now have
available to us, Peter

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:31.840
an extraordinary array
of digital space-based

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:35.580
and other means of tracking
what people are doing.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:38.780
Al Gore has worked on the
development of thing called TRACE

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:41.500
along with climate activists and others.

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:43.110
It's creating an ability to be able

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:46.020
to have real time readout on exactly

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:48.050
what the footprint of a global company is

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:49.480
or of an institution.

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:51.850
I mean, you could from
space focus in on Yale

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:54.690
and you could read out exactly
what Yale's emissions are

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:56.830
at that point in time and extrapolate

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:58.540
so that public shaming,

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:01.470
public accountability is
gonna be one of the things

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:04.750
as people buy into a global endeavor,

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:07.260
we're not able to have force of law

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:10.130
for a number of different reasons.

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:12.820
So this really does rely
on leveraging people

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:15.560
but here's the other big
thing that is happening.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:17.123
And it's really exciting.

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:19.530
Private capital.

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:21.540
There is no government in the world

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:24.600
that has enough money to be
able to close the finance gap

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:26.620
that the UN has identified.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:30.500
There's a one to $2 trillion
per year finance gap

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:32.300
just for the energy sector

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:35.660
in the transition we need to
make in the next 30 years.

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:36.830
So how do you do this?

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:40.760
Well, we've gone to individual
finance institutions.

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:43.660
We've got six of the
largest banks in America.

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:46.630
They've created a floor of a commitment

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:51.390
over the next 10 years that
will put about $4.16 trillion

00:05:55.820 --> 00:06:00.150
to use for climate investment
during that period of time.

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:02.150
And that's without factoring in

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:04.080
a bunch of asset managers

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:05.880
and you take an entity like BlackRock.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:09.030
BlackRock is probably gonna
put another trillion in

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:11.980
maybe two over the course
of 10 years, who knows.

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:14.753
But if you start adding this up,

00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:17.580
the accrued amount of
investment is gonna be

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:19.020
unlike anything we've ever seen

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:20.940
and people are already moving there.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:22.500
Look at Tesla,

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:27.450
the value of Tesla is more
than more than ExxonMobil, BP,

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:29.790
And Chevron put together.

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:32.010
And all that does is make electric cars.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:32.843
Oh, it does.

00:06:32.843 --> 00:06:37.210
I mean, it's not minimalist,
but they're the reefs now

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:41.580
to zero emissions vehicles is intense

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:43.480
and you've got major automobile companies.

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:45.360
GM says they're gonna have no more

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:48.160
internal combustion engines after 2035,

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:50.200
Ford is moving in the same direction,

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:53.560
European car makers moving
in the same direction.

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:58.290
So the momentum of the
marketplace is also gonna provide

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:02.470
a level of impetus as well as
accountability in my judgment.

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:05.040
And people are gonna demand this,

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:08.390
this has taken on thanks to young people

00:07:08.390 --> 00:07:12.260
sort of speaking out and
screaming about the future.

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:15.190
I think finally people
are saying, wait a minute

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:17.950
there's actually money to
be made in this enterprise.

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:20.030
We built out the grid of America.

00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:22.889
We build cars, there's more work.

00:07:22.889 --> 00:07:25.070
We're the fastest growing jobs in America

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:27.790
is wind turbine technician,

00:07:27.790 --> 00:07:32.540
third fastest growing job in
America, solar panel installer.

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:35.780
And so they're gonna be a
lot of changes in our economy

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:36.613
that come from this.

00:07:36.613 --> 00:07:39.010
I think it's the biggest
transformation theater

00:07:39.010 --> 00:07:40.970
since the industrial revolution,

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:45.190
bigger by far than the technology
revolution of the 1990s

00:07:45.190 --> 00:07:49.300
in computer desk, computers
and mobile phones and so forth.

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:51.160
This is gonna be gigantic.

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:53.570
And I think the marketplace realizes it.

00:07:53.570 --> 00:07:58.070
And every boardroom in America
is now grappling with ESG

00:07:58.070 --> 00:08:01.190
considerations, environment,
social and governance.

00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:04.140
And you also have the
sustainable development goals

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:07.010
of the United Nations that
are hanging over people.

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:11.190
So we may finally see a moment
of global responsibility,

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:16.190
but as I said, 45% is
still about signed up

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:18.760
and we've got to move those countries.

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:23.090
And they're big China, India, Russia.

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:25.530
Some of the biggest emitters on the planet

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:27.610
20 countries are the equivalent

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:30.580
of about 75% of all emissions.

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:33.403
We are gonna continue to
work with those 20 countries.

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:37.850
- You were mentioning young people

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:39.530
in your answer that question.

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:41.090
And of course we're a university

00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:43.890
and we have many students who are watching

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:46.863
this symposium today and
participating in Climate Day.

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:52.870
And I'd be interested in your
thoughts about what students,

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:58.870
what younger people can do
to influence Climate Policy.

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:04.320
And in particular, one of
the skills that you need

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:08.210
if you really want to be
someone who works on the policy

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:11.510
front and maybe has a career like yours.

00:09:11.510 --> 00:09:13.890
- Well, I think the
skillset Peter is the same

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:16.590
as skillsets and other disciplines.

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:20.930
I mean, if you're a decent
student in whatever it is you do,

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:23.380
if you want to focus on climate

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:27.173
it's there to be focused on
and we need you desperately.

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:31.990
The fact is that, I mean
Yale is doing great job

00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:34.160
in the various schools of instilling

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:35.650
a lot of interest in people.

00:09:35.650 --> 00:09:39.970
I was privileged in my
fellows and in my seminar

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:43.320
to have people who were just
super motivated and focused,

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:46.370
a couple of whom I bumped
into down here in Washington,

00:09:46.370 --> 00:09:47.760
one's working in the white house,

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:48.840
one's working in state department.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:51.390
I mean, it happens very quickly.

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:55.780
So the fact is that
young people historically

00:09:55.780 --> 00:09:58.700
and I say this with some measure of pride

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:02.380
because in the 1960s, a
lot of us became involved

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:05.077
in the civil rights movement of '64 '65

00:10:05.077 --> 00:10:07.560
and the environment movement,
the women's movement

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:09.830
the peace movement, the war in Vietnam.

00:10:09.830 --> 00:10:10.900
And the first thing I did

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:13.640
I remember it was marching
in and not just marching

00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:16.570
but organizing Earth Day in Massachusetts

00:10:16.570 --> 00:10:18.890
in new England back in 1970,

00:10:18.890 --> 00:10:23.890
that mobilization of voters
is ultimately what happened

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:28.580
was a moment of accountability
based on environment.

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:31.900
And immediately the Congress
passed the Clean Air Act,

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:34.260
Safe Drinking Water,
Marine Mammal Protection,

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:37.370
Coastal Zone Management,
Endangered Species.

00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:40.430
And we created the
Environmental Protection Agency

00:10:40.430 --> 00:10:41.390
of our country.

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:45.290
That came out of young
people, student activism,

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:48.200
people going out and holding
the system accountable

00:10:48.200 --> 00:10:51.670
to a large measure that's
happened already, with respect.

00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:56.670
I think 2020 is the
first election since 1970

00:10:56.870 --> 00:11:00.220
in which environment was
in fact a voting issue

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:02.810
and President Biden seized on it

00:11:02.810 --> 00:11:05.890
had a major proposal in the
context of the campaign.

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:08.680
He's implementing it now, rejoining Paris

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:10.130
leaning on the summit,

00:11:10.130 --> 00:11:14.800
beginning getting a 50 to 52%
target to reduce in America.

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:16.760
I think young folks are
to look at that and say,

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:19.090
gosh, you know we really played a role

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:20.310
in helping to make that happen,

00:11:20.310 --> 00:11:22.550
but boy are we not finished?

00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:25.030
This transition is gonna be a fight

00:11:25.030 --> 00:11:27.703
and we need everybody
involved in this fight.

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:34.000
- Yale has such a long history
of educating young people

00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:36.810
for service to state and community.

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:40.460
And I of course, hope that will continue.

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:42.950
We try to give students as
many opportunities we can

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:47.950
to learn those skills and be
inspired by people like you

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:53.870
and participate in what's
going on in Washington

00:11:53.890 --> 00:11:56.090
or for that matter in
Hartford or New Haven.

00:11:57.900 --> 00:12:00.866
Speaking of politics, what,

00:12:00.866 --> 00:12:05.866
we have such highly
polarized political debate

00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:07.924
in this country now.

00:12:07.924 --> 00:12:10.880
And even climate change,

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:12.060
I say even climate,

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:16.750
is climate change is
highly politicized as well.

00:12:16.750 --> 00:12:19.700
And yet it's gonna really
take a United effort.

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:24.700
It would seem to develop
solutions to address the crisis,

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:28.860
and just the ways that you suggest,

00:12:28.860 --> 00:12:30.940
what can we do?

00:12:30.940 --> 00:12:33.650
And is the role for universities

00:12:33.650 --> 00:12:35.770
in addition to our convening function

00:12:37.070 --> 00:12:39.700
to help bridge these political divides

00:12:39.700 --> 00:12:42.970
and get people on the same
page and not make this yet

00:12:42.970 --> 00:12:47.710
another area where a big
swath of the country simply

00:12:47.710 --> 00:12:49.563
is not interested in engaging.

00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:56.200
- Well, Peter, boy, that's the
big challenge to the country.

00:12:56.200 --> 00:12:57.650
And there's the big question.

00:12:59.950 --> 00:13:03.470
To some measure Yale's motto addresses

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:08.010
that Lux et veritas, we need truth.

00:13:08.010 --> 00:13:12.103
We don't have a baseline for
truth in our system right now,

00:13:14.090 --> 00:13:15.780
people actually have the ability

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:19.063
and have been creating their own facts,

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:22.620
creating their own reality.

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:24.850
Look at what's happening
with President Trump.

00:13:24.850 --> 00:13:26.250
I mean, I don't want to politicize this

00:13:26.250 --> 00:13:30.620
but what's happening in
this context of the big lie

00:13:30.620 --> 00:13:31.753
and the big steel.

00:13:32.870 --> 00:13:36.170
If that's the motivation of
a major party in our country

00:13:36.170 --> 00:13:39.470
we really have a problem in
trying to get things done

00:13:39.470 --> 00:13:41.410
in a bipartisan fashion.

00:13:41.410 --> 00:13:44.960
And the only thing I can
really honestly think of

00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:49.200
to say to people is
there's no automatic pilot

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:50.770
for democracy.

00:13:50.770 --> 00:13:52.340
You don't just cruise along.

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:53.890
You can't cruise along.

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:56.540
You have to fight for what is the truth

00:13:56.540 --> 00:13:57.373
you have to fight right now

00:13:57.373 --> 00:14:00.560
that's been happening
and the demonstrations

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:05.540
and after George Floyd, the
Police Reform Initiative.

00:14:05.540 --> 00:14:07.890
Now other kinds of things are emanating

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:10.800
out of this struggle.

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:13.250
And the victory of President Biden

00:14:13.250 --> 00:14:15.060
is in fact a reflection of that,

00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:16.570
of a large part of that battle.

00:14:16.570 --> 00:14:19.490
He ran on restoring
the soul of our country

00:14:19.490 --> 00:14:23.330
and wanting to restore some norms.

